proofs.proof_tex:
For a fixed eligible pair \((g,k)\), the definition of \(r_{gk}\) is an exact equality.  The eligible estimator and causal target satisfy
\[
 \widehat\tau_k=\sum_{g\in E_k}\frac{n_g}{N_k}\widehat\tau_{gk},
 \qquad
 \tau_k^{\mathrm{event}}=\sum_{g\in E_k}\frac{n_g}{N_k}\tau_{gk}. \tag{1}
\]
The second identity follows from \(N_k=\sum_{g\in E_k}n_g\) and the definition of \(\tau_k^{\mathrm{event}}\).  Subtracting (1), substituting the exact block decompositions, and interchanging finite sums gives
\[
 \widehat\tau_k-\tau_k^{\mathrm{event}}
 =\sum_{q\in\mathcal Q_n^{\mathrm{IF}}}
 \left\{\sum_{g\in E_k}\frac{n_g}{N_k}b_{gk,q}\right\}e_q
 +\sum_{g\in E_k}\frac{n_g}{N_k}r_{gk}. \tag{2}
\]
By \(\texttt{def:staircase-influence-map}\), the braced coefficient is exactly \(c_{kq}\).  Assumption \(\texttt{ass:horizon-variance-nondegeneracy}\) gives \(s_k>0\), and the variance lower bound gives \(\sigma_q>0\).  Since \(a_{kq}=c_{kq}\sigma_q/s_k\), division of (2) by \(s_k\) yields
\[
 s_k^{-1}(\widehat\tau_k-\tau_k^{\mathrm{event}})
 =\sum_q a_{kq}\frac{e_q}{\sigma_q}
 +s_k^{-1}\sum_{g\in E_k}\frac{n_g}{N_k}r_{gk}. \tag{3}
\]
Comparison with the definition of \(\rho_{n,k}\) proves the coordinate formula, and stacking the \(K\) coordinates proves the vector identity.

On \(\mathcal A_n(C)\), the weights \(n_g/N_k\) are nonnegative, so the triangle inequality and the definition of \(\Delta_{\mathrm{CY},n}(K)\) give
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \|\rho_n\|_\infty
 &\le\max_{k\in\mathcal K_n}s_k^{-1}
 \sum_{g\in E_k}\frac{n_g}{N_k}|r_{gk}|\\
 &\le C\max_{k\in\mathcal K_n}s_k^{-1}
 \sum_{g\in E_k}\frac{n_g}{N_k}R_{gk}^{\mathrm{CY}}
 =C\Delta_{\mathrm{CY},n}(K). \tag{4}
 \end{aligned}
\]
Equivalently, with the generic event from \(\texttt{thm:generic-remainder-event-transfer}\),
\[
 \mathcal A_n(C)\subseteq
 \mathcal R_n(C\Delta_{\mathrm{CY},n}(K)),
 \qquad
 P_n\{\mathcal R_n(C\Delta_{\mathrm{CY},n}(K))^c\}
 \le P_n\{\mathcal A_n(C)^c\}. \tag{5}
\]
Thus this theorem supplies the Choi--Yuan specialization \(u=C\Delta_{\mathrm{CY},n}(K)\) to the generic transfer theorem; it does not supply a vanishing probability for the event in (5).

For the finite source-submodel probability statement, apply \(\texttt{lem:choi-yuan-one-block-remainder}\) separately to each eligible block, mapping \((N_o,T_o,\vartheta_o)=(N_{gk},T_g,n_g)\).  Proposition A.4 expands the imputation error for every treated row \(i\in I_g\) into its donor-column term, treated-row pre-period term, and entry remainder \(\mathcal R^M_{gk,i}\).  Since
\[
 Y_{i,g+k}-m^0_{i,g+k}-\tau_{i,g+k}=e_{i,g+k},
\]
subtraction of that expansion and averaging over \(i\in I_g\) gives
\[
 r_{gk}=-\frac1{n_g}\sum_{i\in I_g}\mathcal R^M_{gk,i}. \tag{6}
\]
Hence \(\max_{i\in I_g}|\mathcal R^M_{gk,i}|\le C R_{gk}^{\mathrm{CY}}\) implies \(|r_{gk}|\le C R_{gk}^{\mathrm{CY}}\).  The source submodel imposes the proposition's hypotheses block by block with common constants, so for all sufficiently large \(n\),
\[
 P_n\{|r_{gk}|>C R_{gk}^{\mathrm{CY}}\}
 \le C_0\min\{N_{gk}^{-9},T_g^{-9}\}. \tag{7}
\]
Boole's inequality over the finite eligible-pair set yields
\[
 P_n\{\mathcal A_n(C)^c\}
 \le C_0\sum_{k\in\mathcal K_n}\sum_{g\in E_k}
 \min\{N_{gk}^{-9},T_g^{-9}\}. \tag{8}
\]
Nothing in the event-transfer class forces the right side of (8) to vanish.  This proves exactly the finite union bound and the stated absence of a stronger class-uniform conclusion.

proposed_assumptions.condition:
For every scalar group-at-time functional covered by Choi--Yuan's staggered-adoption Theorem 3.2, using that theorem's typed notation, the signal condition is
\[
 \frac{\sigma\kappa^{23/4}\mu^{3/2}r^{3/2}\sqrt{N_0}
 \max\{N_0\sqrt{\log N_0},T_d\sqrt{\log T_d}\}}
 {\psi_{\min,O_d}\min\{N_0,T_d\}}\to0,
\]

For every such functional, the spectral-size condition in the same source notation is
\[
 \frac{\kappa^{11/2}\mu^3r^3\sqrt{N_0}
 \max\{\sqrt{N_0\log^3N_0},\sqrt{T_d\log^3T_d}\}}
 {\min\{N_0^{3/2},T_d^{3/2}\}}\to0,
\]

For every such functional and every source subgroup \(l\), the missing-block condition is
\[
 \frac{|\mathcal G_l|\kappa^{17/4}\mu^{5/2}r^{5/2}
 \max\{\sqrt{N_0\log N_0},\sqrt{T_{d_l}\log T_{d_l}}\}}
 {\sqrt{N_0}\min\{N_0,T_{d_l}\}}\to0,
\]
